In article <94msom$7ge$1@nnrp1.deja.com>,  <stuart_archer@hotmail.com> wrote:
> Hi all,
> I have a user account that I require to upload files via ftp to a
> directory, but I only want it to have access to this directory, sort of
> like an Rsh for ftp. (incidentally even if I change the user shell ro
> Rsh it still has full file system access via ftp). Anyone got any ideas?

That's because ftpd doesn't invoke a shell, it's doing its own thing.

You have two (possibly three) options (that I'm aware of):

1) rather than having the user upload files to a real account, have the
user ftp in anonymously (this requires that you've done the anonymous ftp
setup on your machine) but provide a place for the user to put the files.
This is commonly done via a writeable

	/pub/hidden/some-directory-name-thats-hard-to-guess

that "only" the appropriate person knows about.  Since they're coming
in anonymously, they only have access to what's in your anonymous ftp
area.

2) Use a non-stock ftpd that has better/stronger/more configurable support
for captive environments.  wu-ftpd and proftpd are the two common ones,
I would recommend proftpd over wu-ftpd.

3) Some stock vendor ftp daemons support forcing someone into a chroot()'ed
environment if the user's password file home directory entry is constructed
in some odd "magic" way, such as

	/path/to/foo/./foo

I'm not sure if the Compaq ftpd supports this, but if it does it should be
documented in the ftpd man page (I haven't looked).

Tim
