Hi again J,

> vm:
> vm-maxvas = 1073741824 (1GB)

As long as you're sure your program won't try to use more, this looks
ok.  How much memory in the workstation, by the way?  (Apologies in
advance if you've already provided this information.)

> proc:
> [...]
> per-proc-data-size = 134217728

I would go ahead and make this 1GB, same as for vm-maxvas and the other
per-proc stuff that's set to 1GB.  With that 134MB setting, you might be
placing a ceiling on the memory you can use.

> maxusers is set to 32, but I don't think it can matter, it is just a
> workstation with no more than 2-3 users logged in.

A common misconception regarding "maxusers" is that it has to do only
with the maximum number of users on the system.  This is not true -- it
actually controls several other important parameters (explained in the
tuning guide).  I would go ahead and kick this WAY up as I said before.


> Before starting
> compilation I make all possible ulimit settings to unlimited, so it
> reads:
>
> core file size (blocks)     unlimited
> data seg size (kbytes)      1048576
> file size (blocks)          unlimited
> max memory size (kbytes)    505688
> open files                  4096
> pipe size (512 bytes)       8
> stack size (kbytes)         32768
> cpu time (seconds)          unlimited
> max user processes          64
> virtual memory (kbytes)     1048576
>

I've never had to do anything with ulimit -- always done it in
sysconfigtab, so I can't comment here, except to ask whether you have
set your soft limits all the way up to what the hard limits will allow?
Have you compared the outputs of "ulimit -aS" and "ulimit -aH"?

> Then I start the compilation and monitor the amount of free memory and
> swap. Some strange things that I've noticed: in spite of the fact that
> there is still free memory, the percentage of used swap also grows as
> memory is allocated. Even when there are tons of free memory (like 40K
> pages), both this memory AND swap are utilized, which I can't explain.
> When the amount of memory occupied by the process (sum of free memory
> plus swap) reaches approximately 150MB, it aborts, saying "Virtual
> memory exhausted" - but there is still 20-30K pages of free memory and
> at least 50% of swap left!!! Tracing the kernel calls shows, that the
> process is aborted when mmap fails with ENOMEM.
>
> The questions now are: why it uses swap, when free memory is still
> available and what limit am I overlooking?
>

You're probably using aggressive (immediate) swap, right?  See the
"swapon" man page for more.  You may be able to get by with lazy
(deferred) swap if no one's going to complain if processes die on the
workstation because they can't get swap space.  Production systems that
can't crash or lose processes due to swap unavailability generally use
immediate swap, but this may not reflect your needs, i.e. you may not
need to be as conservative.

Hope this helps a little more,
WJD


