on Linux;  I use for that the root command as follow:
echo 1 >  /proc/sys/net/ipv4/tcp_abort_on-overflow on linux 2.2.14-12.

but tcp stack accepts all connection and resets them after !!!
My client receives connection reset by peer when receiving data !!

Normally the tcp stack should work as bellow ?
Once the backlog queuebecomes full, no further connection requests will
be accepted for that service until an entry becomes available in the
queue. Under normal conditions, if a connection request is made and the
queue is full, TCP relies on the client to retransmit the connection
request at some later stage. Generally speaking, TCP is quite expedient
in processing connection requests and so, unless the service is
particularly busy, a retransmitted request should be processed.



Could anyone help me. I want to make tcp rejected my incoming request
when the tcp listen queue is full
on Linux, Solaris 2.8, Tru64, HP-UX and AIX.


I have a workaround exposed here after but this solution seems to me
quick and dirty.

Define a threshold of max pending connections the server handles
* server accepts all connection and pushes the created client socket
into a fifo queue of pending connection
   until the fifo becomes full
