I remember reading or being told that it is better to have one large swap
partition versus two disparately sized partitions. e.g. swap1 being 50M and
swap2 being 8G. As the method of paging tries to write equally between the
swap partitions, therefore when swap1 fills up, the alogrithm wastes time
trying to write to it, before writing to swap2.

Granted it's probably not a great overhead, but on a system that is thrashing
swap it could be a problem. Also I could be mistaken in this belief. Obviously
two swap partitions the same size, on different disks, is better than one.

