Keith Bradnam wrote:

> Hi,
>
> Like many other people (or so it seems) I've started noticing that our
> Tru64 system (running Apache with mod perl) keeps generating defunct
> processes that seem to be connected to the httpd processes that are
> generated.
>
> I can remove them by killing the parent httpd process and they don't
> *appear* to be doing any harm but can anyone explain to me why they
> appear, and maybe what I need to do about them (if anything).
>
> Many thanks,
>
> Keith
>
> ~  Keith Bradnam - UK CropNet (http://ukcrop.net) webmaster and
> ~  Arabidopsis Genome Resource  (http://ukcrop.net/agr) developer.
> ~
> ~  Nottingham Arabidopsis Stock Centre, University Park,
> ~  University of Nottingham, NG7 2RD, UK.  Tel: (0115) 951 3091

Hi,

defunct process come about by the httpd forking off child processes. The
child process then finishes it's business and sends a sig{chld} to the
parent process. Which is meant to have a signal handling procedure to reap
it's children. Obviously your httpd process isn't doing this. You may need
to update to the latest version of apache.

Having them hang around doesn't really suck up any CPU resources, it does
take up an entry in the process table. So you may start hitting limits
based around max-proc-per-user etc... It is not really good procedure to
have them hanging around.

Robert Mulley
Unix admin



