In article <93i94h$717$1@nnrp1.deja.com>, timjarland@my-deja.com writes:
>
>I have inherited an Alpha 4100 server running DEC Unix 4.0F.  The
>system also attaches to a Digital 450 RAID Array.  The previous SA has
>configured the arrayd using advfs.  There are now space issues on the
>box.

>How can I list the available physical volumes?

On the array connect a terminal to the console port of (one of)
the controller(s) and do a "show device".  Also count the number
of disks in the subsystem.  If there are more physical disks
than shown in the "show device" listing, you add the remainder
by hand.  Having a copy of the controller CLI guide would be
useful.

To see how the device are associated with logical units and
storage sets, use the commands "show unit" and "show storageset".

>How can I scan the RAID array for available disks to configure
>as physical volumes?

If a device isn't used by a logical unit or storage set, that
clear from the show device listing:

HSZ> show device

Name          Type                      Port Targ  Lun        Used by
------------------------------------------------------------------------------

DISK100       disk                         1    0    0        S1
DISK110       disk                         1    1    0        D100
DISK120       disk                         1    2    0

In this example, DISK100 is used by the storage set S1.  DISK110
is used by the logical unit 100.  DISK120 is unused.

From the host side, you can look at the boot listings to see what
devices were there when it booted.  The system probably has one
or more devices not connected through the array.  These could
be connected through a simple SCSI adapter, or a backplane array
controller.  To see the devices known by the SCSI driver, use
the scu(8) command.

>Can an existing advfs file system span multiple physical volumes?

Yes.  You need to have the AdvFS Utilities license.  The command
to add volumes to a domain is addvol.

