In article <3A59D463.319B23A5@m1a2r3.dfo-mpo.gc.cax>, James Hanway <hanwayj@m1a2r3.dfo-mpo.gc.cax> writes:
> This is probably more of a generic UNIX question:
> 
> I placed executables into a directory that is on the global PATH for all
> users. Users that are logged in at the time cannot "see" the next
> executables until they either re-login or issue a "rehash" command in
> their csh terminal windows. Why?
> 

  It depends on the shell.  csh hashes the data it finds in the PATH
  when it starts, and doesn't really search the PATH unless you tell it
  "rehash".  Other shells actually search PATH for every command.  If a
  user was to start a new csh process, that one should pick up the new
  command since it will hash on start.

  As always in UNIX, it depends.

