Hello.

I am sorry if this is not the right forum; I could not find many
newsgroups related to Alpha.

I am trying to get some performance measurements for programs running
on Alpha.  What I would like to do is to count the execution time of a
program EXCEPT in a few portions of code.  I have the source code of
the programs I am measuring.  I am interested in being as much
"cycle-accurate" as possible.  I am using 'gcc -O4'.

I have tried to use ATOM, but it is too heavyweight: the code I am
measuring is executed often, and the overhead of inserting procedure
calls is too big (1000%).

Now I have resorted to inserting just a few instructions to read the
cycle counter register (using rpcc), and to make simple computations
with it.  I get the counters before and after the code I want to skip,
and I accumulate only the time spent outside this code.

My programs don't run too much, so the counters don't overflow.

However, I encounter two problems with this approach:

1) the results vary from measurement to measurement, even on an
   unloaded system (around 10% variance I would say).

2) I believe that the compiler and the OOO execution actually reorder
   my instrumentation code, and thus I am not really measuring what I
   want.

My questions are:

a) is there a method to measure in a very lightweight fashion just the
   user-level runtime of a process?  should I expect for such a value
   a variance of an order >10%, due to the unpredictable scheduling on
   OS and application code on the CPU?

b) is there a way to avoid the reordering of the instrumentation
   instructions, without incurring a big overhead (i.e. >10%)?

c) is there some documentation with guidelines on how to carry such
   measurements on Alphas (or perhaps any other Unix system)?

Thank you very much for any hint.
Mihai Budiu



