YMMV.  The system may work faster if you move most of the swap space to
a different drive since there tends to be lots of I/O requests for the
root drive.  If you're not swapping a lot, or if your other drive is
heavily loaded with I/O requests you may see no change or worse
performance.

IMHO measure first with a tool like collect or monitor for a few days
and then see what you should really do.  I have all my swap space on the
root drive just because my performance needs are met.

IMHO vendor "technical" folks just sometimes know that isn't the way
they're in the habit of doing it and think therefor nobody should.

